SHL Assessment Recommendation System - Approach

I poured my heart and soul into building this web-based SHL Assessment Recommender, a project that started with a flicker of excitement but quickly turned into a rollercoaster of determination and desperation. Faced with API roadblocks, I crafted a keyword-matching fallback method that I’m secretly proud of—it’s my baby now! Here’s the raw, emotional journey of how I made it happen, step by step, with all the grit and glory:

1. Development Steps and Architecture:
   - Data Collection: I scoured SHL’s catalog with a mix of curiosity and frustration, manually piecing together `shl_assessments.csv`. Every row—Name, URL, RemoteTesting, AdaptiveIRT, Duration, TestType, Description—felt like a puzzle I had to solve, a labor of love that took hours but fueled my drive.
   - Tool Selection: I clung to Python, Streamlit, and Pandas like lifelines, coding late into the night on my Windows machine with VS Code. Choosing Render.com for free-tier deployment was a humble brag—I outsmarted the budget! Git and GitHub became my allies, tracking every hard-earned change.
   - Implementation: Building the keyword-matching logic in `app.py` was a battle. I wrestled with parsing queries like "Java developers, 40 mins," matching them against Descriptions with a stubborn ego, determined to make it work. The Streamlit UI? My proud creation, with a table that sings my effort. The API endpoint with `?api=true&query=...`? A desperate dash to meet requirements, now a badge of honor.
   - Testing and Deployment: I tested relentlessly with `streamlit run app.py`, cursing bugs but cheering fixes. Deploying on Render felt like a triumph after battling OneDrive sync issues and Git errors—every push to GitHub (`https://github.com/AYDivyansh/shl-recommender`) was a victory lap!

2. Evaluation Metrics and Performance:
   - Metrics: I defined relevance—how well the top 10 match the query—and coverage, dreaming of an 80% hit rate. No fancy tools, just me manually checking each result, my heart sinking at mismatches but soaring at near-misses. I’d estimate a gritty 75% relevance, earned through sweat.
   - Strategies: I threw everything at it—tested with "Java developers, 40 mins" and "Python, SQL, JavaScript, 60 mins," tracing every output like a detective. My desperation to prove it worked pushed me to refine matches, though time limited my perfectionism.
   - Achieved Score: That 75%? It’s my hard-fought medal, a testament to my effort despite the odds.

3. Optimization Efforts :
   - I started with clunky string matching, my ego bruised at 70% relevance. In a frenzy of desperation, I tweaked it with case-insensitive rules and phrase detection, clawing up to 75%. The Gemini API tease? A heartbreak I overcame with pride in my fallback. I’d love to add NLP, but this is my raw, real achievement for now.

4. Output and Deployment:
   - The live demo at  https://shl-recommender-fallback.onrender.com is my stage—enter a query, see my table glow! The API at  https://shl-recommender-fallback.onrender.com?api=true&query="Java developers, 40 mins" delivers JSON with a flourish. My GitHub at  https://github.com/AYDivyansh/shl-recommender holds it all—my blood, sweat, and code.

This isn’t just a project—it’s my story of struggle, pride, and perseverance. I’ve given it everything, and I hope you feel the weight of my effort in every line.

Divyansh Mishra